 "notes": "Libretto by Lorenzo Da Ponte (1790). Words follow the Peters engraving's spelling and punctuation: rumor (the libretto has romor; IPA ruˈmor), sung by Despina too; vò; “io vò guardar” without a the second time; tornaro (the libretto has tornano; “they have come back”, torˈna.ro); “Gli sposi vostri…” and “Lo sposo mio…” with one “…” where the print has “...”; li (Despina and Don Alfonso: them) and ci (Ferrando and Guglielmo: us). The soldiers' chorus off stage (bars 1–13, “Bella vita militar”) sings in the score and is heard with the piano, but has no role and no lines here. IPA, word meanings and translations come from the app's reviewed Così libretto drafts (L65, IPA checked against Castel), repeated where the score repeats the text; repeated fragments have short literal translations. IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, elisions (two words on one note in the score) joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ. Translation literal, for meaning.",
